  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1055 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nMuch quieter across the region tonight with just a few \nlingering showers clipping extreme southeast NE as a shortwave \ntrough continued to pass through KS. At the surface, high \npressure continued to build in from the north, with the mid to \nhigh level clouds tied to the wave to the south slowly, but \nsurely exiting to the southeast. Some lower clouds across SD \ninto northern NE were trying to fill in behind them, but were \ngenerally dissipating as they pushed farther southeast. As a \nresult, expecting mostly clear skies for a good chunk of the \narea overnight. Given how wet we are, can't completely rule out \nsome fog development under the clear skies, but guidance isn't \nshowing much of anything, owing to decently strong winds aloft \nand drier air moving in. So if anything does develop, it would \nlikely be pretty patchy and/or shallow.\n\nThe other news in the short term is continued flooding on area \nrivers, creeks, and streams. Most locations outside of the \nMissouri River have or are close to cresting. With no additional\nrain in the short term forecast, current river forecasts should\nbe in good shape, with minor to moderate flooding continuing.\n\nOtherwise, it's looking like a pretty quiet next 7 days as we \nremain under zonal to northwest flow aloft. We'll see several \nfrontal passages through the period, starting with a cold front \nSaturday night into Sunday, a warm front around Tuesday, then \nanother cold front Wednesday into Thursday. Guidance is in \npretty good agreement that said frontal passages will be dry, \nwith the best chances being with the cold front toward mid-week,\nthough a vast majority of ensemble members still keep us dry, \nwith chances remaining under 10%. Despite the fronts, \ntemperatures won't fluctuate a whole lot. Expect highs in the \n60s to lower 70s through Monday, followed by mostly 70s Tuesday \nthrough the remainder of the work week.",
